# Core Rules

Authority: `docs/context/` is canonical. `docs/research/` is superseded history;
never implement from it.

## Non-negotiable

1. Deterministic versioned code owns every live decision: signals, sizing,
   allocation, risk, order creation, stops, exits, flattening.
2. AI is asynchronous and proposal-only. It never calls a broker, mutates live
   state or config, or bypasses a gate. No LLM call in a live decision path.
3. Strategies emit `TradeIntent` only. They never construct broker orders.
4. Layer 2 recalculates every financially authoritative value at decision time.
5. Broker orders, fills, positions and funds are external truth.
6. Unknown, stale, inconsistent or invalid critical state blocks new exposure.
   Existing positions keep deterministic protection regardless.

## Layer ownership

| Layer | Owns | Never |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 Data | Feeds, quality state, features, IV/Greeks | Trade selection, sizing |
| 2 Risk | Portfolio, sizing, limits, OMS, exits, reconciliation | Setup detection |
| 3 Strategy | Setup logic, requested risk, exit template | Final qty, broker calls |
| 4 Analytics | Evaluation, proposals, backtest | Any live mutation |

Domain and calculation code imports no infrastructure. Side effects live behind
typed ports.

## Configuration, not constants

Lot sizes, order-rate caps, margin rules, expiry penalties, loss caps and
freshness thresholds are validated configuration. Never a literal in domain
logic, never a remembered value. Unverified config fails closed.

## Money and time

`Decimal` or integer minor units for anything financial; never `float`. All
timestamps timezone-aware, stored UTC, with exchange-local session logic.
Time and IDs are injected, never read from ambient global state.

## Style

Python 3.12, type hints mandatory, `mypy --strict` clean. Polars over Pandas.
One-line docstrings. Comments explain why, not what. Patch minimally; do not
reformat untouched code.
